  <h2>3. Matter Summary</h2>
  <p>Mrs Borji reports that she was not made aware of the potential structural, mortgage, property value or future roof-access implications associated with spray foam loft insulation at the point of sale.</p>
  <p>She further reports that the spray foam insulation was installed negligently and to an unacceptable standard, resulting in significant damage to the property. A subsequent inspection by a local roofing company reportedly identified excessive humidity levels within the loft and extensive deterioration of the timber roof structure.</p>
  <p>Mrs Borji states that she has photographic evidence of the damage. Owing to the severity of the deterioration, a substantial section of the roof required replacement. Spray foam insulation remains present on the loft floor, joists and a dividing brick wall.</p>

  <h2>4. Preliminary Assessment</h2>
  <div class="box">
    On the information currently available, the potential case appears to focus on unsuitable sale, failure to warn, negligent installation, lack of adequate roof suitability assessment, lack of ventilation risk warnings, and damage caused or contributed to by the spray foam installation.
  </div>
  <p>The case would be strengthened by evidence showing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Who supplied, sold and installed the spray foam insulation.</li>
    <li>What survey or roof suitability check was carried out before installation.</li>
    <li>What warnings were given about moisture, ventilation, timber deterioration, mortgageability, saleability and future roof repairs.</li>
    <li>What the Green Homes Scheme voucher covered and whether any scheme standards, warranties or installer obligations applied.</li>
    <li>Whether a roofing contractor or surveyor can link the humidity/timber deterioration to the spray foam condition or installation method.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Potential Responsible Parties to Review</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Party</th><th>Reason to review</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Protect Insulation Services Ltd</td><td>Potential supplier/installer route. First complaint should normally be addressed to the company that sold and/or installed the spray foam.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Green Homes Scheme / grant administrator</td><td>Funding route and possible scheme standards or approved-installer requirements. The grant voucher should be checked carefully.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Warranty or guarantee provider</td><td>If any installation warranty, insurance-backed guarantee or scheme guarantee exists, it may provide a route for complaint or evidence.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roofing contractor / surveyor</td><td>Not a defendant based on current information, but essential evidence source for causation and loss.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>6. Estimated Loss Position</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Loss item</th><th>Amount</th><th>Evidence required</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Spray foam installation / grant-funded works</td><td>GBP 7,350</td><td>Contract, quote, invoice, Green Homes Scheme voucher, grant payment evidence.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roof replacement / remedial works</td><td>GBP 5,800</td><td>Roofing invoice, quote, payment proof, photographs and contractor report.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Current preliminary direct loss</td><td>GBP 13,150</td><td>Subject to evidence, causation, limitation and recoverability.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>7. Recommended Next Steps</h2>
  <div class="green">
    <ol>
      <li>Obtain the Protect Insulation Services Ltd contract, quote, invoice, survey notes and any warranty paperwork.</li>
      <li>Obtain and review the Green Homes Scheme grant voucher and any scheme correspondence.</li>
      <li>Request a written roofing report confirming humidity, timber deterioration, what works were required and whether spray foam contributed to the damage.</li>
      <li>Collect photographs of the loft, joists, dividing brick wall, spray foam remaining on the loft floor and roof timbers.</li>
      <li>Send a formal complaint to Protect Insulation Services Ltd requesting documents, liability response and reimbursement.</li>
      <li>Check whether any warranty, guarantee, scheme administrator or insurance-backed route exists.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2>8. Documents Still TBC</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Full installer company address and company number.</li>
    <li>Green Homes Scheme voucher and payment evidence.</li>
    <li>Roofing company name, report and invoice.</li>
    <li>Photographic evidence set.</li>
    <li>Any guarantee, warranty, sales brochure or pre-installation survey.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="small"><strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This report is an initial assessment based on information supplied. It is not legal advice and does not guarantee recovery. Further review depends on documents, limitation, causation evidence and the response of the relevant parties.</p>
